A||||3||Richard Parnell||Oil pressure sensor oil  leak||||9||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||12-18-2000||01:12 PM||||Got a problem that maybe someone can help with. Not long ago after we did the V8 conversion on my 87 sc, I found that the car was leaking oil.  It took me a little while but it was the oil pressure sensor leaking.  Right out the end where the electrical connectors are.  I bought a new one and darned if that one hasn't started leaking.  The reading on the guage is around 50-60 .  What do I do to fix this problem?||||reg||9
Z||000001||batboy||12-18-2000||03:44 PM||||It's not unusual for an old Chevy small block oil sending unit to do that, but it does seem odd that a new one would leak so quick. Probably a defective part. Try another one that is a name brand and see what happens.||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||12-18-2000||10:10 PM||||...that, or the new sender got damaged in some way while being installed.  If so, might break down and buy a socket made for the purpose.  Not sure what your situation is, of course - it's just a thought.<P>The oil pressure switch (for idiot light) on my 1971 Cutlass 350 V8 developed a small leak once, like BB mentioned.  It drooled down the front of the engine onto the crank pulley, making me think the front crank seal might be going away.  My brother told me, "Check the oil pressure switch first, before you do anything."   He was right  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The plastic part on top of it just started leaking - probably it just got brittle over the years and developed a crack.  Replaced the switch, and the leak was gone  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Richard Parnell||12-19-2000||01:11 PM||||Guys- I'm using the same sensor as I had with the 4 cyl.  The new one is leaking right out from where the electrical connectors are.  The pressure reading with the V8 is about 50, it was 80 with the old 4 banger.||||reg||1
